# coord release nightly-window — fires coord-release-window.service
# (Type=oneshot) once a night. See that unit's header for the full
# rationale (#2112, rewritten #2587).
#
# 10:00 America/Chicago — #2866: this is what the fleet actually runs
# (dellserver carried it as an untracked
# `coord-release-window.timer.d/override.conf` drop-in from 2026-08-26
# until this became the packaged default), replacing the earlier packaged
# 03:00 UTC. Named with an explicit zone rather than a bare local wall-clock
# time specifically so a DST transition cannot drift the fire time by an
# hour twice a year — `systemd.time(7)`'s `OnCalendar=` resolves a
# zone-qualified spec against that zone's own clock, DST included. Still
# inside a low-drive-activity part of the day; the exact hour matters far
# less than picking ONE the operator can reason about, since #2587 already
# means a late/early firing costs nothing (see below). #2587: this firing
# itself is now fast (resolve the target, set-or-fire the roll-pending
# marker, return) — there is no multi-hour drain to leave room for any
# more. The ACTUAL roll may still land well after this fires if the fleet
# stays busy; that is fine and by design, since it fires the instant `coord
# drive-queue tick` sees the fleet's own next inter-drive gap rather than
# waiting for THIS timer's next fire, and never stops any timer to get
# there.
#
# The dellserver drop-in this superseded can now be deleted — this
# `OnCalendar=` reproduces it exactly (same zone-qualified spec), so
# removing the drop-in changes nothing. A from-scratch rebuild of that host
# would otherwise have silently reverted to 03:00 UTC with no warning,
# which is the whole reason this moved from operator-local override to
# packaged default.
#
# RandomizedDelaySec is small and exists only to avoid firing at the exact
# same wall-clock instant `coord-drive-queue.timer`'s own OnCalendar-free
# interval schedule might land on, not because two hosts might race this
# unit — it belongs on the daemon host ONLY (same rule as
# coord-release-propagate.timer; see that unit's header for why a second
# copy elsewhere is the #1440 two-overseers failure in a new costume).
#
# Persistent=true (#2112 trap 4 / acceptance-adjacent): a host that was
# asleep or rebooted through 03:00 still catches up on the next boot, rather
# than silently skipping a whole night. `coord release nightly-window`
# itself is idempotent — see its module docstring's `needs_roll`: a fleet
# already current at whatever time this actually fires touches nothing at
# all.

[Unit]
Description=Nightly release window on the daemon host (#2112, #2587)

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 10:00:00 America/Chicago
RandomizedDelaySec=5min
AccuracySec=1min
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
